./agent-runs/results/9-kernel-mismatch.stream.jsonl:{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"d863feba-81c6-4592-bd83-6eaf2848a29d","status":"SUCCESS","response":"### F5 — Make Kernel Mismatch a Fatal Composition Problem: Complete

#### 1. Summary of Changes
- **Fatal Kernel Mismatch Enforcement in Composition:**
  - In [`src/cdn/methods/release.ts`](file:///home/ubuntu/code/mesh-serve-dispatch-9/src/cdn/methods/release.ts#L55-L125), added `'kernel_mismatch'` to [`CompositionProblem['kind']`](file:///home/ubuntu/code/mesh-serve-dispatch-9/src/cdn/methods/release.ts#L55-L58) and defined [`KernelCheck`](file:///home/ubuntu/code/mesh-serve-dispatch-9/src/cdn/methods/release.ts#L60-L64).
  - Updated [`checkComposition()`](file:///home/ubuntu/code/mesh-serve-dispatch-9/src/cdn/methods/release.ts#L78-L125) to verify each part's declared kernel range against the release's resolved kernel version using pure [`satisfies`](file:///home/ubuntu/code/mesh-serve-dispatch-9/src/catalog/methods/semver.ts#L129-L142) and [`parse`](file:///home/ubuntu/code/mesh-serve-dispatch-9/src/catalog/methods/semver.ts#L25-L36). If a declared range does not satisfy the kernel, it emits a fatal [`kernel_mismatch`](file:///home/ubuntu/code/mesh-serve-dispatch-9/src/cdn/methods/release.ts#L119-L124) problem naming both the part and the declared range (e.g. `theme requires kernel ^0.6, but this release serves 0.13.0.`).
  - Updated [`isFatal()`](file:///home/ubuntu/code/mesh-serve-dispatch-9/src/cdn/methods/release.ts#L127-L129) to treat `kernel_mismatch` as fatal (the same class as `missing_part` and `unmet_contract`).
- **Caller Wiring in CDN Compose:**
  - In [`src/cdn/tools/compose.ts`](file:///home/ubuntu/code/mesh-serve-dispatch-9/src/cdn/tools/compose.ts#L48-L100), recorded `version.kernel` when resolving parts from the catalog and forwarded `{ version: resolved.kernel.version, ranges: kernelRanges }` to [`checkComposition()`](file:///home/ubuntu/code/mesh-serve-dispatch-9/src/cdn/methods/release.ts#L78-L125).
  - Any fatal problem refuses composition, returning `hash: ''` and leaving the database untouched.
- **Catalog Schema & Documentation:**
  - In [`src/catalog/schema/part.ts`](file:///home/ubuntu/code/mesh-serve-dispatch-9/src/catalog/schema/part.ts#L193-L205), updated the doc comment on [`PartVersionSchema.kernel`](file:///home/ubuntu/code/mesh-serve-dispatch-9/src/catalog/schema/part.ts#L193-L205) documenting its compose-time enforcement and absent-range semantics.
  - In [`spec/roadmap.md`](file:///home/ubuntu/code/mesh-serve-dispatch-9/spec/roadmap.md#L421-L428), ticked **F5** as complete.
  - In [`spec/unread.md`](file:///home/ubuntu/code/mesh-serve-dispatch-9/spec/unread.md#L53), updated `partVersion.kernel` to strike-through and marked it resolved in F5.

---

#### 2. Key Architectural Decisions & Report Findings

Full report available at [`agent-runs/reports/9-kernel-mismatch.md`](file:///home/ubuntu/code/mesh-serve-dispatch-9/agent-runs/reports/9-kernel-mismatch.md):

1. **What an absent range means on a part, and the argument:**
   - **Decision:** Accepted without error (unconstrained / not a mismatch).
   - **The Argument:** Versions published before the `kernel` field was introduced have none. Because catalog versions in `mesh-serve` are immutable (`PartVersionSchema` enforces that published commits and versions cannot be modified), refusing unstated ranges would retroactively break every existing release composed from legacy parts. Furthermore, reporting a non-fatal warning on every compose creates permanent, un-actionable noise for site operators composing releases from third-party catalog packages. The proper boundary to enforce that `kernel` is declared is at **publish time** (`publish-cli` / `catalog.publish`) on the part author's side; at **compose time**, the engine enforces declared constraints and accepts unconstrained parts.
2. **Whether any release currently in the database would now be refused:**
   - **Finding:** **Exactly zero.**
   - Inspection of the live database (`mesh-serve` on MongoDB `localhost:27017`) confirms the clean rebuild earlier today left 0 releases, 0 sites, and 0 partVersions. Test run databases (`mesh-serve-spine-*`) only contain releases where kernel is `0.3.0` and parts declare `^0.3` (satisfied). No release in the database is refused.
3. **Whether `checkComposition`'s four problem kinds are still the right set, now that there are five:**
   - The five kinds (`missing_part`, `missing_optional`, `unmet_contract`, `unused_grant`, `kernel_mismatch`) correctly reflect the domain invariants.
   - Importantly, `cdn.compose` only checks parts and kernel at compose time because releases are site-independent (C1, D4); contracts (`unmet_contract`, `unused_grant`) are deploy-time invariants checked against `site.mesh` during `cdn.deploy`.

---

#### 3. Verification
- **Unit Tests in [`test/cdn/release.test.ts`](file:///home/ubuntu/code/mesh-serve-dispatch-9/test/cdn/release.test.ts#L155-L242):**
  - Refuses kernel 0.13 with a part declaring `^0.6`, naming both the part and the range.
  - Refuses when kernel version is partial string `0.13`.
  - Treats a kernel artifact's own absent requirement as not a mismatch.
  - Accepts an absent range on a part without error.
  - Satisfied when kernel is within declared range (`0.11.4` with `^0.11`).
  - Reports kernel mismatch alongside missing parts and contract errors simultaneously.
- **Integration Tests in [`test/integration/spine.test.ts`](file:///home/ubuntu/code/mesh-serve-dispatch-9/test/integration/spine.test.ts#L427-L476):**
  - End-to-end `cdn.compose` refuses an out-of-range part (`fixture-stale` declaring `^0.2` on kernel `0.3.0`), returning `hash: ''` and emitting `kernel_mismatch`.
  - End-to-end `cdn.compose` composes cleanly when a part declares no kernel range (`fixture-legacy`).
- **Suite Status:** 28/28 test files passed, 352/352 tests passing.
- **Typecheck:** `npm run typecheck` clean, **zero type casts**.
- **Commits on `dispatch/9`:**
  - [`74162a3`](file:///home/ubuntu/code/mesh-serve-dispatch-9): `F5: make kernel mismatch a fatal composition problem`
