-mismatch.prompt.txt\\r\\nnew file mode 100644\\r\\nindex 0000000..d5a4ee5\\r\\n--- /dev/null\\r\\n+++ b/agent-runs/prompts/9-kernel-mismatch.prompt.txt\\r\\n@@ -0,0 +1,96 @@\\r\\n+Code lives in:  FLYBYME/mesh-serve — this worktree. All commits go here.\\r\\n+\\r\\n+================================================================================\\r\\n+THE TASK\\r\\n+================================================================================\\r\\n+\\r\\n+**F5 — `partVersion.kernel` is stored and never read.** Rated **S**. Make a\\r\\n+kernel mismatch a fatal composition problem.\\r\\n+\\r\\n+`publish-cli` writes the range a part was built against, with the comment *\\\"the\\r\\n+only thing standing between a stale part and a browser\\\"*. `build_start` forwards\\r\\n+it. **Nothing reads it.** `checkComposition` checks missing required parts, unmet\\r\\n+contracts and unused grants, and has **no kernel case at all**.\\r\\n+\\r\\n+Add a `kernel_mismatch` problem, **fatal** — the same class as a missing required\\r\\n+part, not a warning like an unused grant.\\r\\n+\\r\\n+================================================================================\\r\\n+IT IS NOT THEORETICAL, AND HERE IS THE MEASUREMENT\\r\\n+================================================================================\\r\\n+\\r\\n+The roadmap entry cites one historical violation. Measured against the live\\r\\n+platform on 2026-09-06, before that day's rebuild, **five of six live sites were\\r\\n+serving parts outside their declared range**:\\r\\n+\\r\\n+| site | kernel served | parts declaring |\\r\\n+| --- | --- | --- |\\r\\n+| `demos.localhost` | 0.11.4 | `theme` `clock` `notes` `calc` — all `^0.6` |\\r\\n+| `todo.localhost` | 0.11.4 | `todo` `^0.6` |\\r\\n+| `demo.localhost` · `localhost` · `127.0.0.1` | 0.11.4 | `auth@0.2.0` `^0.6` |\\r\\n+| `console.localhost` | 0.11.4 | `chrome` `catalog` `releases` `^0.11` ✓ |\\r\\n+\\r\\n+`^0.6` is `\\u003e=0.6.0 \\u003c0.7.0`. Every one of those compositions should have been\\r\\n+refused and none was.\\r\\n+\\r\\n+**And when the range was finally enforced by hand**, bumping `mesh-demos` from\\r\\n+`^0.6` to `^0.13` immediately failed to compile: `workbench` had narrowed the\\r\\n+window mode to `'windowed' | 'tiled'` in four places and the kernel had gained\\r\\n+`'single'`. That is a real incompatibility this check would have surfaced at\\r\\n+compose time instead of leaving it to a browser.\\r\\n+\\r\\n+================================================================================\\r\\n+WHERE IT GOES\\r\\n+================================================================================\\r\\n+\\r\\n+    src/cdn/methods/release.ts       checkComposition — the distinctions are the\\r\\n+                                     point: a required part absent **refuses**,\\r\\n+                                     an optional one **reports**, an unmet\\r\\n+                                     contract **refuses**, an unused grant\\r\\n+                                     **reports**. A kernel mismatch refuses.\\r\\n+    src/catalog/schema/part.ts       partVersion.kernel, and its comment\\r\\n+    src/cdn/tools/compose.ts         the caller\\r\\n+    spec/unread.md §1                the `partVersion.kernel` row, which this\\r\\n+                                     closes — update it to say what happened,\\r\\n+                                     the way the `release.exposure` row was\\r\\n+                                     updated by D4\\r\\n+\\r\\n+**A kernel artifact has no kernel requirement of its own** — the one thing that\\r\\n+has no kernel. `DeclarationSchema.kernel` is `.optional()` for exactly that\\r\\n+reason. An absent range is not a mismatch.\\r\\n+\\r\\n+**Decide what an absent range means for a *part*, and say why.** Versions\\r\\n+published before the field existed have none. Refusing them breaks every existing\\r\\n+release; accepting them silently is how the field became decorative in the first\\r\\n+place. There is a third option — accept, and report it as a non-fatal problem —\\r\\n+and it may be the right one. Argue it rather than picking quietly.\\r\\n+\\r\\n+================================================================================\\r\\n+WHAT GOOD LOOKS LIKE\\r\\n+================================================================================\\r\\n+\\r\\n+- **A test composing kernel 0.13 with a part declaring `^0.6`, refused, naming\\r\\n+  both the part and the range.** That is the whole item.\\r\\n+- A test that a kernel artifact's own absent requirement is not a mismatch.\\r\\n+- A test for whatever you decide an absent range means on a part.\\r\\n+- A test that a satisfied range composes cleanly — the six live sites are all in\\r\\n+  range as of the rebuild, so a false positive here breaks production.\\r\\n+- Every existing test passing. `npm run typecheck` clean, **zero casts**.\\r\\n+- Tick F5 in `spec/roadmap.md`; update the `partVersion.kernel` row in\\r\\n+  `spec/unread.md` §1.\\r\\n+\\r\\n+**Run every command in the foreground. Commit as you go.**\\r\\n+\\r\\n+================================================================================\\r\\n+YOUR REPORT\\r\\n+================================================================================\\r\\n+\\r\\n+`agent-runs/reports/9-kernel-mismatch.md`\\r\\n+\\r\\n+- what an absent range means on a part, and the argument\\r\\n+- **whether any release currently in the database would now be refused.** The\\r\\n+  platform was rebuilt clean earlier today so the answer should be none — if it\\r\\n+  is not none, that is the finding and it matters more than the code.\\r\\n+- whether `checkComposition`'s four problem kinds are still the right set, now\\r\\n+  that there are five\\r\\n+- anything for `spec/unread.md`\\r\\n\"}}}\n./agent-runs/results/9-kernel-mismatch.stream.jsonl:{\"event\":\"step_update\",\"step_update\":{\"conversation_id\":\"93523d3b-46df-4ef0-8404-ed18864cbbdd\",\"st